<section>
 <div class="container">
  <h2>The STAR Framework (Behavioral Questions)</h2>
  <p>Use this for behavioral questions: "Tell me about a time when..."</p>

  <div style="background:rgba(110,231,255,.08);border:1px solid rgba(110,231,255,.2);border-radius:16px;padding:30px;margin:30px 0">
   <h3 style="margin-top:0;color:var(--accent)">STAR = Situation • Task • Action • Result</h3>
   <ul style="list-style:none;padding:0;margin-top:20px">
    <li style="margin:15px 0"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Situation:</strong> <span style="color:var(--muted)">Set the scene. Where were you? Who was involved? What was the context?</span></li>
    <li style="margin:15px 0"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Task:</strong> <span style="color:var(--muted)">What was the challenge? What did you need to accomplish?</span></li>
    <li style="margin:15px 0"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Action:</strong> <span style="color:var(--muted)">What specific steps did YOU take? Focus on your contribution.</span></li>
    <li style="margin:15px 0"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Result:</strong> <span style="color:var(--muted)">What was the outcome? Use numbers if possible: "improved by 30%", "delivered on time"</span></li>
   </ul>
  </div>

  <h3>Example STAR Answer:</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
   <strong>Q: "Tell me about a time you failed and what you learned"</strong><br><br>
   <strong>Situation:</strong> "In my sophomore year, I was building a React app for a class project with two teammates. We didn't plan well and everyone was working on different things."<br><br>
   <strong>Task:</strong> "We had 2 weeks left but the code was a mess and nothing integrated together."<br><br>
   <strong>Action:</strong> "I took ownership and reorganized our workflow. I set up Git properly, created a component structure, and held daily 15-min sync-ups with the team. I also documented what each person should build."<br><br>
   <strong>Result:</strong> "We finished on time and got an A. More importantly, I learned that planning upfront saves 10x work later. I now always spend the first 20% of projects planning."
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section>
 <div class="container">
  <h2>Common Behavioral Questions</h2>
  <p style="color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:20px">Prepare STAR stories for these. Have 2–3 strong examples ready.</p>

  <div class="qna">
   <h4>1. "Tell me about yourself"</h4>
   <p>60–90 seconds. Your background, what you're interested in, why this role matters to you. Practice this out loud.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="qna">
   <h4>2. "Why do you want to work here?"</h4>
   <p>Research the company. Name specific projects, values, or problems they solve that excite you.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="qna">
   <h4>3. "Tell me about a time you failed"</h4>
   <p>Show self-awareness. Don't make it sound like a humble brag. Focus on what you learned.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="qna">
   <h4>4. "Describe a challenge you overcame"</h4>
   <p>Show problem-solving. Use STAR. Be specific about YOUR actions, not your team's.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="qna">
   <h4>5. "How do you handle disagreement with a teammate?"</h4>
   <p>Show maturity. Explain how you listen, find common ground, and prioritize the team's goals.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="qna">
   <h4>6. "What's a project you're proud of?"</h4>
   <p>Pick a real project. Explain the problem, your role, and the impact. This is your chance to shine.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="qna">
   <h4>7. "What are your strengths and weaknesses?"</h4>
   <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Be specific. "I'm detail-oriented — I caught 3 major bugs during code review last month."<br>
   <strong>Weaknesses:</strong> Pick something real but not critical for the role. Show you're working on it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="qna">
   <h4>8. "Where do you see yourself in 5 years?"</h4>
   <p>Don't say "your CEO." Instead: "Senior engineer" or "Technical lead" or "Startup founder." Show ambition.</p>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section>
 <div class="container">
  <h2>Technical Interview Tips</h2>
  
  <h3>For Coding Problems:</h3>
  <div class="timeline">
   <div class="timeline-item">
    <h4>1. Understand the Problem (2 min)</h4>
    <p>Ask clarifying questions. "Is the array sorted?" "Can values be negative?" Shows you think deeply.</p>
   </div>
   <div class="timeline-item">
    <h4>2. Think Out Loud (2–3 min)</h4>
    <p>Explain your approach before coding. Interviewers care about your thinking process, not just the code.</p>
   </div>
   <div class="timeline-item">
    <h4>3. Start Simple (5–10 min)</h4>
    <p>Write a brute-force solution first. Then optimize. Shows you understand fundamentals.</p>
   </div>
   <div class="timeline-item">
    <h4>4. Code Carefully (10–15 min)</h4>
    <p>Write clean, readable code. Use meaningful variable names. Explain what you're doing as you go.</p>
   </div>
   <div class="timeline-item">
    <h4>5. Test Your Code (3–5 min)</h4>
    <p>Walk through an example. Test edge cases: empty input, single element, duplicates.</p>
   </div>
   <div class="timeline-item">
    <h4>6. Discuss Complexity (2 min)</h4>
    <p>Talk about time and space complexity. Can you optimize further?</p>
   </div>
  </div>

  <h3>What Interviewers Actually Care About:</h3>
  <div class="grid" style="margin-top:20px">
   <div class="card">
    <div class="icon">🧠</div>
    <h3>Problem Solving</h3>
    <p>Can you break down complex problems? Do you think methodically?</p>
   </div>
   <div class="card">
    <div class="icon">💬</div>
    <h3>Communication</h3>
    <p>Can you explain your thinking? Do you ask good questions?</p>
   </div>
   <div class="card">
    <div class="icon">🛠️</div>
    <h3>Code Quality</h3>
    <p>Is your code clean and readable? Do you handle edge cases?</p>
   </div>
   <div class="card">
    <div class="icon">📈</div>
    <h3>Growth Mindset</h3>
    <p>Can you take feedback? Do you iterate and improve your solution?</p>
   </div>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

